{
  "segment_function": "To analyze how the calamities and frailty of human action lead historically and philosophically to the substitution of making and rule (monarchy and tyranny) for genuine action, beginning with Plato.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The calamities of action originate from human plurality, prompting attempts to abolish this plurality through forms of one-man rule or monarchy that banish citizens from the public realm.",
      "claim_type": "author_position",
      "supporting_excerpt": "The calamities of action all arise from the human condition of plurality, which is the condition sine qua non for that space of appearance which is the public realm. Hence the attempt to do away with this plurality is always tantamount to the abolition of the public realm itself. The most obvious salvation from the dangers of plurality is mon-archy, or one-man-rule...",
      "support_kind": "direct",
      "confidence": 1.0
    },
    {
      "claim": "Political philosophy since Plato has largely served as an attempt to escape from the frailty of politics and action into quiet, order, and the concept of rule.",
      "claim_type": "author_position",
      "supporting_excerpt": "Escape from the frailty of human affairs into the solidity of quiet and order has in fact so much to recommend it that the greater part of political philosophy since Plato could easily be interpreted as various attempts to find theoretical foundations and practical ways for an escape from politics altogether.",
      "support_kind": "direct",
      "confidence": 1.0
    }
  ],
  "definitions": [],
  "distinctions": [],
  "references_to_other_sections": [],
  "unresolved_context": [],
  "must_not_be_lost": [
    "Plato's separation of archein (beginning) and prattein (achieving) transforms the political realm into a hierarchy of rulers who command and subjects who merely execute orders."
  ]
}

